   - Training data for actual cell detection at correct magnification

8. Current Work:
   The conversation ended while discussing the hardware/optics requirements for real-time cell visualization. The user confirmed they want judges to use a cotton swab on a slide and see their own cells detected live, but immediately revealed that **TSA rules prohibit any liquids** — which eliminates the recommended methylene blue staining approach. No solution was proposed before the summary was requested.

9. Optional Next Step:
   The most critical unresolved issue is the no-liquids constraint for cell visualization. The user's last message was:

   > "fuck we cant have any liquids though, thats part of the TSA thing."

   The next step is to respond to this constraint by proposing viable no-liquid alternatives for cell visualization, such as:
   - **Pre-fixed/dried slides** (cells fixed with ethanol/methanol then air-dried — no liquid at time of use, but still shows real cells; judge could still prep their own slide at a station before the liquid evaporates)
   - **Pre-stained commercially prepared slides** (cheek cell slides exist, cells are permanently stained and dry)
   - **Phase contrast or darkfield** with unstained cells (difficult at low cost)
   - **Re-scoping the demo** — use a different sample type that doesn't require liquid (e.g. dry pollen, pre-dried blood smear)
   This directly addresses the user's last message and is the blocking issue before any further hardware/optical work can proceed.

